---
import Shell from "../layouts/Shell.astro";
import ContactBlock from "../components/ContactBlock.astro";
import { getContent } from "../lib/content";
const { profile } = await getContent();
---

<Shell
  title="About — Salman Asif"
  description="Senior full-stack engineer with a frontend foundation and cross-stack ownership across enterprise systems, mobile products, services, and delivery."
  ><section class="page-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">THE HUMAN IN THE SYSTEM</div><h1>
      I care how it works.<br />And how it <em>feels.</em>
    </h1><p>{profile.intro}</p>
  </section><div class="about-story page-content">
    <div class="about-sidebar">
      <span class="eyebrow">CURRENT COORDINATES</span><strong
        >{profile.location}</strong
      ><span>Senior Software Engineer<br />Veeam · Securiti AI</span><span
        class="eyebrow">ALWAYS EXPLORING</span
      ><span
        >Creative WebGL<br />Realtime systems<br />AI product ideas<br
        />Developer workflows</span>
    </div><article class="prose">
      <h2>Start with the whole picture.</h2><p>
        I’m Salman, a full-stack engineer with a strong frontend foundation. The
        work I enjoy most sits between product behavior, service contracts,
        state, and the small interface details that make a difficult workflow
        feel obvious.
      </p><p>
        My path has taken me from startup React products and React Native apps
        to enterprise privacy and data-security systems. Along the way, I’ve
        learned that a reliable interface starts well before the component: in
        the contract, the state model, the failure paths, and the decisions
        about what happens next.
      </p><h2>Fix the cause. Keep the system clear.</h2><p>
        When a production issue crosses several layers, I follow it. That can
        mean tracing Vuex state, checking an API payload, working through a Go
        or Python service response, or investigating a difference in a
        deployment environment.
      </p><p>
        I like architecture that a team can keep working with. Reusable
        patterns, explicit state, and a smaller change surface are often more
        valuable than an impressive abstraction that only one person
        understands.
      </p><h2>Make room for curiosity.</h2><p>
        This garden is a place to explore the more playful side of that
        thinking. Three.js, motion, and interactive systems let me turn
        otherwise invisible connections into something tangible.
      </p><p>
        I’m interested in problems that need someone to move comfortably between
        product questions and implementation detail — and stay with the work
        through review, release, and the next iteration.
      </p><a href="/cv" class="text-link">The complete professional story →</a>
    </article>
  </div><ContactBlock profile={profile} /></Shell
>
